    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e3568085-4011-5bd8-b176-01e3f3d29e06",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=12 e^{- \\frac{y}{4}},\\qquad 3 x - 1>0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=4 \\ln{\\left(3 x - 1 \\right)}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f\\prime(x)=\\frac{16}{3 x - 1}.$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=4 \\ln{\\left(3 x - 1 \\right)},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(4 \\ln{\\left(3 x - 1 \\right)}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=4\\cdot\\frac{3}{3 x - 1} \\\\ &=\\frac{12}{3 x - 1} \\\\ &\\not\\equiv \\frac{16}{3 x - 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=4 \\ln{\\left(3 x - 1 \\right)}$ and $y\\prime=\\frac{12}{3 x - 1}$ into the original equation gives an identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}12 e^{- \\frac{y}{4}} \\\\ \\frac{12}{3 x - 1} &\\overset{?}{=}12e^{-(4 \\ln{\\left(3 x - 1 \\right)})/(4)} \\\\ \\frac{12}{3 x - 1} &\\overset{?}{=}12e^{-\\ln(3 x - 1)} \\\\ \\frac{12}{3 x - 1} &=\\frac{12}{3 x - 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=4 \\ln{\\left(3 x - 1 \\right)}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
